WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.976 --> 00:00:06.301
BEN: Hi, Little Fox readers, and welcome back to People in the News . Today we'll

2
00:00:06.302 --> 00:00:12.784
be meeting one of the world's most brilliant scientists, Professor Stephen Hawking. OLIVIA: Hello, everyone! Stephen was

3
00:00:12.785 --> 00:00:22.881
born on January 8, 1942, in Oxford, England, to a highly educated family. His father was

4
00:00:22.882 --> 00:00:32.533
a doctor, and his mother was one of the first women to attend Oxford University, a very

5
00:00:32.534 --> 00:00:40.875
prestigious school. Despite being a smart kid, Stephen didn't learn to read until he was eight,

6
00:00:40.876 --> 00:00:51.512
and he got terrible grades in school. He was more interested in creating intricate board games with

7
00:00:51.513 --> 00:00:57.067
his friends, and when he was 16, he and his friends built a computer out of

8
00:00:57.068 --> 00:01:07.578
spare parts. That was in 1958, before personal computers even existed! Stephen's father hoped he would become

9
00:01:07.579 --> 00:01:13.402
a doctor, but Stephen was more interested in science. He was particularly fascinated by the night

10
00:01:13.403 --> 00:01:18.464
sky. He and his family would lie outside on clear nights to gaze up at the stars.

11
00:01:18.465 --> 00:01:28.220
The stars filled Stephen with a sense of wonder about the universe. Even though his grades

12
00:01:28.221 --> 00:01:38.048
were not the best, people recognized Stephen's talents, and he was admitted to Oxford University at the

13
00:01:38.049 --> 00:01:43.308
age of 17. After Oxford, Stephen went to Cambridge University to study cosmology, the branch of

14
00:01:43.309 --> 00:01:49.369
science that deals with the whole universe. His goal was to understand everything about how the universe

15
00:01:49.370 --> 00:01:54.550
works. When Stephen was a student at Oxford, he'd noticed some disturbing physical changes. He had

16
00:01:54.551 --> 00:02:01.612
become a bit clumsy, and he started to slur his speech. These symptoms got worse during his

17
00:02:01.613 --> 00:02:10.542
first year at Cambridge, and his parents convinced him to see a doctor. After three weeks

18
00:02:10.543 --> 00:02:19.258
of tests, doctors told Stephen he had amyotrophic lateral sclerosis, or ALS. It's a fatal disease that

19
00:02:19.259 --> 00:02:23.929
destroys the nerves that control muscle function. It gets progressively worse, and there's no cure. Stephen

20
00:02:23.930 --> 00:02:28.275
was told he'd only live another two and a half years. BEN: Wow . . . How

21
00:02:28.276 --> 00:02:33.654
did he handle that terrible news? OLIVIA: At first Stephen was devastated, and he grew very

22
00:02:33.655 --> 00:02:41.089
depressed. But then he decided to make the most of the time he had left, and he

23
00:02:41.090 --> 00:02:51.331
devoted himself to his research. He soon realized that he actually liked studying, and he was

24
00:02:51.332 --> 00:03:03.836
able to develop some very important theories that made him famous in the scientific community. Stephen managed

25
00:03:03.837 --> 00:03:10.391
to outlive his doctors' prediction about his life expectancy, but as his fame grew, his body

26
00:03:10.392 --> 00:03:17.225
deteriorated, and he was eventually confined to a wheelchair. Then in 1985 Stephen lost his voice after

27
00:03:17.226 --> 00:03:21.467
an emergency tracheotomy. He couldn't use his hands to type, so his only way to "talk"

28
00:03:21.468 --> 00:03:25.804
was to have people show him words. When he saw the word he wanted to use, he'd

29
00:03:25.805 --> 00:03:30.643
raise his eyebrows. Communicating like that was very difficult and time-consuming. What made it even worse

30
00:03:30.644 --> 00:03:38.127
was that he was in the middle of writing a book. Fortunately a computer programmer heard

31
00:03:38.128 --> 00:03:44.777
about his situation and sent him a program that could speak for him. Stephen could select words

32
00:03:44.778 --> 00:03:51.443
from a computer screen, and then the computer would say what Stephen had written. The voice

33
00:03:51.444 --> 00:03:59.022
sounded robotic, and it had an American accent, but Stephen had a voice again. It also enabled

34
00:03:59.023 --> 00:04:06.689
him to finish his book, A Brief History of Time , which was published in 1985.

35
00:04:06.690 --> 00:04:10.772
Stephen hoped the book would make complicated theories about cosmology understandable to people who were not scientists.

36
00:04:10.773 --> 00:04:16.254
He also hoped it would encourage more interest in science. The book was a huge bestseller

37
00:04:16.255 --> 00:04:24.175
and turned Stephen into a celebrity. He did guest appearances on a couple of TV shows and

38
00:04:24.176 --> 00:04:34.400
became one of the most famous scientists in the world. Unfortunately, Stephen's condition continued to get

39
00:04:34.401 --> 00:04:40.908
worse, and soon the only part of his body that he could move was his cheek muscle.

40
00:04:40.909 --> 00:04:44.896
A small sensor was attached to his eyeglasses, which detected the movements of his cheek. This

41
00:04:44.897 --> 00:04:58.815
became his only way of communicating with anyone. Stephen has said that he's lucky he became interested

42
00:04:58.816 --> 00:05:07.228
in cosmology because, unlike other branches of science, cosmology doesn't require any hands-on experiments—Stephen works entirely

43
00:05:07.229 --> 00:05:10.732
in his head. Most of his work consists of complicated mathematical equations that Stephen has to memorize

44
00:05:10.733 --> 00:05:20.950
since he can't write them down. BEN: So what exactly does he research? OLIVIA: He's particularly

45
00:05:20.951 --> 00:05:25.964
interested in black holes. A black hole is an infinitely small and dense point in space, also

46
00:05:25.965 --> 00:05:31.033
called a singularity. It can occur when a really big star dies and caves in on

47
00:05:31.034 --> 00:05:38.515
itself. The pull of its gravity is so strong that light can't even escape! Stephen believes the

48
00:05:38.516 --> 00:05:47.445
entire universe started as a singularity that exploded in an event called the Big Bang. From

49
00:05:47.446 --> 00:05:52.848
that explosion everything in the universe was eventually created—galaxies, stars, planets, even us! Stephen has lots of

50
00:05:52.849 --> 00:05:58.093
other ideas that are usually only found in science fiction. For instance, he believes people will

51
00:05:58.094 --> 00:06:02.938
colonize other planets someday. And he doesn't think we should try to contact aliens from another planet.

52
00:06:02.939 --> 00:06:13.490
He says that any aliens who were able to travel the distance needed to get here

53
00:06:13.491 --> 00:06:18.567
would have to be so technologically advanced they'd be downright dangerous to us! Stephen has also hypothesized

54
00:06:18.568 --> 00:06:26.058
that time travel will be possible someday. But don't get too excited, Ben. You won't be

55
00:06:26.059 --> 00:06:31.715
able to go back in time to fix your mistakes on math tests. According to Stephen, we'll

56
00:06:31.716 --> 00:06:35.949
only be able to go forward, into the future. BEN: What an incredible mind, and what

57
00:06:35.950 --> 00:06:41.558
an inspiring story. Stephen has accomplished so much despite having a very debilitating disease. Well, that's it

58
00:06:41.559 --> 00:06:50.960
for now, Little Fox readers. We'll see you all in the future—next week, to be precise!

